# Cloudflare Pages reads this file at deploy time and attaches these
# response headers to every request it serves. See:
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/
#
# The whole site is static HTML/CSS/JS — no server, no database, no
# cookies, no user accounts — so most of the classic web attack surface
# (SQL injection, server-side RCE, session hijacking, auth bypass) simply
# does not apply. What IS worth locking down at this layer is the browser
# side: what a malicious or compromised script could do if one ever got
# onto the page, and how the site can be embedded or framed by others.
#
# Content-Security-Policy, directive by directive:
#   default-src 'self'      baseline: only this origin, for anything not
#                            covered by a more specific directive below.
#   script-src               only same-origin script FILES (telaventis.js,
#                            telaventis-fx.js, moka-lab.js, the aurelia.js
#                            dynamic import — all self-hosted, nothing from
#                            a CDN) plus one exact inline script, allow-
#                            listed by its SHA-256 hash rather than a blanket
#                            'unsafe-inline': the single
#                              document.documentElement.classList.add('js')
#                            line every page opens with. No 'unsafe-eval'
#                            on purpose — the one thing on this site that
#                            wants it is the optional WebGPU jellyfish in
#                            assets/aurelia/aurelia.js (Three.js's shader
#                            node system calls `new Function` internally);
#                            that whole load is already wrapped in a
#                            .catch() that falls back to the plain 2D
#                            canvas version (see telaventis-fx.js), so
#                            blocking it here just means every visitor
#                            quietly gets the 2D fallback instead of a
#                            script-level escape hatch staying open
#                            site-wide. Re-add 'unsafe-eval' here if you'd
#                            rather keep the WebGPU version and accept that
#                            trade-off.
#   style-src 'unsafe-inline' the one deliberate exception: the page design
#                            leans on inline style="" attributes throughout
#                            (not a CSS file to swap in). CSS injection is
#                            real but far lower severity than script
#                            injection — no arbitrary JS execution — so it
#                            stays allowed while script-src stays strict.
#   img-src / font-src       self-hosted only (assets/, incl. the woff2
#                            font files); data: kept for img-src as a small
#                            safety margin for inline data-URI assets.
#   connect-src / form-action  'self' plus api.web3forms.com specifically:
#                            the contact form's only network call, needed
#                            for the fetch() in telaventis.js AND for the
#                            <form action> itself (form-action is its own
#                            CSP directive, separate from connect-src).
#   frame-ancestors 'none'  refuses to be embedded in an <iframe> anywhere,
#                            including this same site — the modern,
#                            enforced-by-CSP replacement for X-Frame-Options
#                            (kept below too, as a fallback for the very
#                            few browsers that only understand the old
#                            header). Blocks clickjacking outright: nothing
#                            legitimately needs to iframe this site.
#   object-src 'none'       no Flash/plugin content, ever.
#   base-uri 'self'         stops a <base> tag injected via any future bug
#                            from silently rewriting every relative link/
#                            script/asset on the page to another origin.
#   upgrade-insecure-requests  belt-and-braces on top of Cloudflare's own
#                            automatic HTTPS.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-Du+OJKJSbdUgz5nrHeWWINvez6XKDDU/tyj/5c2uvwo='; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://api.web3forms.com; form-action 'self' https://api.web3forms.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; upgrade-insecure-requests

# Every file in assets/ is either content-hashed by convention (the
# ?v=NN query string bumped by hand on every edit — see the <link>/<script>
# tags in any page) or genuinely static (fonts, art, icons). Either way the
# URL changes the moment the content does, so caching it "forever" on the
# CDN and in visitors' browsers is safe and meaningfully faster on repeat
# visits — a page-speed signal search engines do factor in.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
